The message queue keeps its place at the readable size, and sits where the
maintainer put it (ROCKNIX fork #295, #296; the seventh cut).

The box follows the font, as upstream draws it: the sixth cut scaled the box
down with the placement and put 14 px of text in 31 px of box. The room under
the bottom box is a constant in placement line heights (4/3: 22 px on a
640x480 panel, where upstream's padding*4 put 42 before the floor and 57
under it), taken from an 11 px reference so that both of RetroArch's layout
passes at a launch clamp to the same value -- the sixth cut's 9 px reference
placed a message pulled between the passes 14 px low and jumped it when the
next message arrived, because gfx_widgets_layout re-measures the messages on
screen and never re-places them. Every box above the bottom one sits one
divider up: the pitch upstream's quarter-height spacing gives when the height
divides by four, without the rounding that ceilf'd the sixth cut's seam away
(a 62 px height made a 31.5 pitch under a 31 px box) and without the first
cut's placement spacing under the floor's box (a 36 pitch under 40: the boxes
overlapped by four rows). Measured per build at the H700's widget scale in
docs/qa-frames/2026-09-27 on the fork. The two log lines say what the font
was, what was chosen, and what the placement became.

